00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:43.030
And then what do you think about saying this?

643
00:33:43.030 --> 00:33:43.110
Yes.

644
00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:44.630
And it was like, oh my gosh.

645
00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:47.170
It was just like this uplifting, exciting.

646
00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:47.890
That's so nice.

647
00:33:48.190 --> 00:33:49.230
Oh, I loved it.

648
00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:49.770
I loved it.

649
00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:51.310
We've talked a lot about that.

650
00:33:51.490 --> 00:33:53.870
And Jen Salotta was very similar as well.

651
00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:58.450
Like, well, Jen, you couldn't get, she just would laugh the whole time.

652
00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:00.870
Like, we'd actually have to throw her off because.

653
00:34:01.110 --> 00:34:01.910
She was laughing too much.

654
00:34:01.910 --> 00:34:03.610
She would ruin takes, but yeah.

655
00:34:03.910 --> 00:34:04.150
Yeah.

656
00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:13.330
I, I thought we, you know, there's this, this thing that we would do on the show where when a cut would come out, the director's cut, and I've never seen it on any other show,

657
00:34:13.330 --> 00:34:16.210
we would all gather and watch it together in the writer's room.

658
00:34:16.470 --> 00:34:20.870
And director's cuts famously in other shows like make you want to kill yourself.

659
00:34:21.050 --> 00:34:25.590
They're usually so bad, but the quality was so good because the cast was so good.

660
00:34:25.690 --> 00:34:28.630
And so I remember it was like my favorite time.

661
00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:33.650
We could interrupt all of our work and just go into the little living room area of our writer's room and watch.

662
00:34:33.730 --> 00:34:34.990
I mean, I was such a fan of the show.

663
00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:40.210
I, I watched almost every cut of every episode and then I would watch it live too.

664
00:34:40.390 --> 00:34:45.190
Like I, and I didn't realize how unusual that is for somebody who works on a show.

665
00:34:45.370 --> 00:34:46.650
Most people do it.

666
00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:49.390
And then they, a lot of actors don't even watch it when it airs.

667
00:34:49.810 --> 00:34:55.850
Um, but I, I really was such a huge fan of the show.

668
00:34:55.930 --> 00:35:01.250
And a lot of times in those conference room scenes, like I'd have like no lines or one line or something in these four page scenes.

669
00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:08.290
And to me, it was like going to second city and just watching everyone just be so funny and it just be for free.

670
00:35:08.410 --> 00:35:11.930
And you'd be in this like freezing cold room and there'd be snacks every two hours.

671
00:35:12.050 --> 00:35:13.730
And I was like, what, how could this be better?

672
00:35:14.730 --> 00:35:17.250
It felt like such an abundance of riches.

673
00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:21.130
I just loved sitting and watching everyone in character, just reacting.

674
00:35:21.510 --> 00:35:21.670
Yeah.

675
00:35:22.090 --> 00:35:24.870
Mindy, I remember one time you were on stage, um,

676
00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:35.750
and BJ and you guys were at reception by Jenna and you guys were talking and I walked up and you were having a conversation about some type of like economics.

677
00:35:35.790 --> 00:35:39.590
It was like some kind of like you'd, you'd expanded economics.

678
00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:43.110
And I walked up and I was like, I'm going to go over here.

679
00:35:43.110 --> 00:35:47.350
I was just like, they are two of the smartest people I've ever met.

680
00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:51.010
And I'm going to hard pivot and back away.

681
00:35:51.410 --> 00:35:53.370
Wait, BJ and I were talking about economics.

682
00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:54.130
We weren't fighting.

683
00:35:54.370 --> 00:35:55.370
I'm so surprised.

684
00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:57.910
Oh no, you were, it was a heated discussion.

685
00:35:58.070 --> 00:35:59.650
You were on opposite sides.

686
00:35:59.990 --> 00:36:01.050
That is so funny.

687
00:36:02.070 --> 00:36:10.970
Well, I just, I remember you and Paul and BJ as a little trio, probably because you were the writers who were with the actors.

688
00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:17.750
So between takes, you guys would always kind of huddle up and talk like story ideas and like what's happening in the writer's room.

689
00:36:17.990 --> 00:36:20.370
And so you were having to kind of manage two jobs at once.

690
00:36:20.410 --> 00:36:32.630
And I used to like hover around you guys and eavesdrop and just like hope you would invite me in to your hilarious conversation.

691
00:36:32.630 --> 00:36:41.370
Or yes, some of my favorite memories are listening to you guys discuss story and character and pitch jokes to each other in between scenes.

692
00:36:41.550 --> 00:36:47.490
Well, that's so funny because I felt like when we were in the annex, we, you know, we were, the writers were in the annex.

693
00:36:47.590 --> 00:36:50.690
I'm sure you're sure you guys have talked about this many times, but we were in the annex, obviously,

694
00:36:50.810 --> 00:36:55.310
because then we could go be the writer's room and we wouldn't have to be in some of the big group scenes.

695
00:36:55.310 --> 00:36:59.370
But I remember also thinking like, but I want to be in the big group scenes.

696
00:36:59.510 --> 00:37:05.550
Like I, like those were the funnest scenes to watch when like, you know, Dwight would run in and be like, everyone to the roof.

697
00:37:05.690 --> 00:37:10.410
